// Title: Persistence Via Sticky Key Backdoor
// Sigma ID: 1070db9a-3e5d-412e-8e7b-7183b616e1b3
// Status: test | Level: critical
// Author: Sreeman
// Date: 2020-02-18 | Modified: 2023-03-07
// ATT&CK: persistence, T1546.008, privilege-escalation
// Description: By replacing the sticky keys executable with the local admins CMD executable, an attacker is
//   able to access a privileged windows console session without authenticating to the system. When
//   the sticky keys are "activated" the privilleged shell is launched.
// False positives:
//   - Unlikely
// References:
//   - https://www.fireeye.com/blog/threat-research/2017/03/apt29_domain_frontin.html
//   - https://www.clearskysec.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/ClearSky-Fox-Kitten-Campaign-v1.pdf
//   - https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/archive/blogs/jonathantrull/detecting-sticky-key-backdoors
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/05d1031f537e830b76da53bae2400c5814c89165/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_cmd_sticky_keys_replace.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, live Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where ProcessCommandLine contains "copy " and ProcessCommandLine contains "/y " and ProcessCommandLine contains "C:\\windows\\system32\\cmd.exe C:\\windows\\system32\\sethc.exe"
